    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Subsurface seismic and coal mine data have been used to investigate the 3D geometry of segmented normal faults. Individual segments in normal fault zones may be either truly discontinuous or they maybe part of a larger, branching fault. The evolution and geometry of relays between fault segments changes not only in time, with increased fault slip, but also in space. Fault slip variations observed in relays along active and ancient faults can be explained by mechanical fault interaction. Truly isolated echelon segments behave similarly to a single larger fault, but can be recognized by irregular slip distributions in the central fault area and multiple closures in the footwall. Branching faults may also exhibit this at some levels, but may have regular slip distributions and a single closure at other levels. Along branch fault segments,fault slip can be abnormally high in comparison with the horizontal fault length. Correct identification and interpretation of these geometries is important to assess spill point locations and closure size. A model is presented that maybe helpful to assess fault sealing potential in terms of fault geometry and slip. It is important to get access to subsurface seismic data to better constrain the modeling and improve the understanding of segmented normal faults.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
